# Angular and TypeScript

Apply this rule to components, templates, and application services. `project-architecture.mdc` owns layer imports.

Use strict TypeScript. Prefer inference when the type is obvious. Use `unknown` instead of `any`. Explain any remaining cast in a short comment.

Follow the defaults of the installed Angular version. Current Angular defaults to standalone components and `OnPush`, so do not set `standalone: true` or `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` on new components. On a version where those are not defaults, set both.

- Declare inputs, outputs, and models with `input()`, `output()`, and `model()`. Mark them `readonly`.
- Inject dependencies with `inject()`.
- Put host bindings in the decorator `host` object.
- Use `@if`, `@for`, and `@switch`.
- Bind `class` and `style` directly. Import only the pipes and directives a template uses.
- Name files in hyphen-case and match the class (`user-profile.ts`, `user-profile.html`).
- Keep template-only members `protected`. Move derived template values into `computed()`.

## Forms, state, and screens

For business forms, use Signal Forms from `@angular/forms/signals` when that package is stable in the installed Angular version. Otherwise use reactive forms. Do not use template-driven forms for business forms. Share a validation rule across the screens that use it instead of copying it.

Keep feature state in that feature with signals. Add a global state library only after a documented requirement.

A component calls an application use case and renders loading, success, empty, and error. Do not use `console.log` as the error path. What an error may reveal is defined in `security-multitenancy.mdc`.

Register a singleton with the root provider of the installed version: `@Service` where Angular provides it, otherwise `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })`.
